VAR_CURRENT measurement probe, 2026-08-30 (Task B, Blocker 2)
Question: at each existing PHYS-ALLOC call, what does VAR_CURRENT
(0x28044) actually hold?  Read, not reasoned.

=== Method ===
Scratch edit (never committed) at the top of PHYS-ALLOC in
forth/dict/hardware.fth:
    BASE @ >R HEX ." CUR=" 28044 @ U. R> BASE !
Probe image build/combined.img sha256:
    38b52e560987ffc61134d7dda981142c52d57352c583ab37091e77b72a3ddd7b
(the probe image is deleted; this hash is the only remaining
evidence the two images differed).  Pre-probe baseline sha256
captured BEFORE the edit:
    bfc2ca00f29794c46dd929c2d849de52e7700be120bc02e8503fa38091b5e4d9
Boot output captured with -serial file: (TCP wait=off discards
output emitted before a client connects -- first attempt lost the
boot lines; file capture records from power-on).
SURVEYOR-DEEP block range derived from tools/write-catalog.py
(blocks_needed + place_vocab walk): blocks 1041..1057.

=== Pre-registered predictions (stated before the run) ===
1. Boot shows exactly 7 CUR= lines: 4 from ahci.fth (CL-BUF,
   FIS-BUF, CT-BUF, SEC-BUF), 2 from rtl8168.fth (TX-DESC,
   TX-BUF), 1 from ntfs.fth (MFT-BUF).
2. Each value = the allocating vocab's OWN LATEST-cell address
   (cross-read at the prompt via `X DEFINITIONS HEX 28044 @ U.`);
   three distinct values.
3. NEGATIVE: none of the boot values is 28048 (VAR_FORTH_LATEST).
   This is the leg that would have caught the pun at boot.
4. Run-time call: ONLY FORTH DEFINITIONS, ALSO SURVEYOR-DEEP,
   DA-ALLOC from the prompt shows CUR=28048 -- the pun firing:
   SURVEYOR-DEEP is the consumer, FORTH gets the tag.

=== Observed ===
Boot (7 lines, in order):
  CUR=33EFC  CUR=33EFC  CUR=33EFC  CUR=33EFC   (ahci x4)
  CUR=36578  CUR=36578                          (rtl8168 x2)
  CUR=37AA0                                     (ntfs x1)
Prompt cross-reads of each vocab's LATEST cell:
  AHCI DEFINITIONS    -> 33EFC
  RTL8168 DEFINITIONS -> 36578
  NTFS DEFINITIONS    -> 37AA0
  ONLY FORTH          -> 28048
Run-time call:
  ALSO SURVEYOR-DEEP  DA-ALLOC  -> CUR=28048
All four predictions confirmed, including the negative leg.

=== Reading ===
All four existing callers allocate at LOAD time under their own
DEFINITIONS: VAR_CURRENT is a truthful owner tag for everything
in the tree today.  The prompt-side run shows the pun is alive:
any run-time allocation (the xHCI hot-plug case this task serves)
lands tagged FORTH -- confidently wrong.  Design consequence
(mentor ruling): FORTH's cell (28048) is a distinct third state,
UNATTRIBUTED, never folded into a vocabulary's total.  Mechanism,
not policy: PHYS-ALLOC compares the captured tag against
VAR_FORTH_LATEST; the gate asserts no driver-initiated allocation
ever matches it.  Operator allocations from the prompt stay
permitted and are reported on their own PHYS-AUDIT line.  A
driver allocation landing unattributed is a FAILURE, not a
footnote.  Keep CURRENT as the observable; the return-address
scheme waits for a run-time caller to test against.

Instrument note: the first capture attempt (TCP, wait=off)
silently discarded everything emitted before the client
connected -- zero CUR= lines.  Against prediction 1 ("exactly
7"), that read as a broken instrument; without the pre-registered
count, a naive read is a finding ("no allocations happen at
boot") -- a confident wrong conclusion from a silent capture
path.  A pre-registered count is what distinguishes an
instrument failure from a finding.

